{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies entirely on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a non-probabilistic basis for preference, but it fails to address the fatal objection that verisimilitude itself cannot be defined or compared without probabilistic assumptions. As noted in the target's own 'counterconditions', if verisimilitude collapses into hidden induction (per Tichý and Miller), the entire mechanism of 'Critical Preference' disintegrates. The target asserts the mechanism works but offers no argument to refute the specific condition that would invalidate it, effectively assuming the solution to the very problem posed (that a non-inductive basis exists) without demonstrating it against the known philosophical refutation.","premise":"The target claims preference is defensible via verisimilitude while admitting this fails if verisimilitude relies on probability.","premises_essential":["fe984010bd04bc79addd09af662604360e4b2ddd563dee8258f172876e5a450e"],"successor_question":"Can you articulate a definition of verisimilitude that allows for the comparison of two unfalsified theories without implicitly relying on inductive assumptions about the uniformity of nature or probabilistic content?","target":"fe984010bd04bc79addd09af662604360e4b2ddd563dee8258f172876e5a450e"}]}